#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"}] :question/id #uuid "055422e3-fb8a-5579-ab97-b946d5687e30" :question/math-academy-id "e-9052" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x \\geq 1.$  When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $(2x-2) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2,$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $\\sqrt2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1\\,\\rm{m},$ find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/0c/0c47a1e988a75e4813c92a76acbf3a821364995123f3eb394b07d4bfaa95f80d.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(2x-2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -(2x-2).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -(2x-2).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int (-2x+2) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=1\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}+2x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =-(1)^{2}+2(1)+C \\\\ C & =1+1-2 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = -x^2+2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =-{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution of this equation for $x \\geq 1$ is $x=2\\,\\rm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/id #uuid "a4d988d6-4181-588c-8e11-02a96554a76a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9053" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m},$ the velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ and the acceleration is $2kx\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1 \\,\\rm{m},$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/62/6280c4b1561cda0d16c6758bb5d8fae30d66d9925bd1bb9db1a29e70093c6f61.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2kx\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2 kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int 2kx \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = kx^2 + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =k(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = kx^2 + 2.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=4\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=1\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(1)^{2}+2 \\\\ 8 & =k+2 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "58325462-3a69-575e-bdfb-31db815b0d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9456" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x \\, \\rm{m},$ the velocity is $v \\, \\rm{m/s},$ and the acceleration is $k(5 - x) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = \\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ at the origin and has velocity $v = 2\\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $1 \\, \\rm{m},$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/c1/c129b3157bf3741f4e1fa30c7bb97eaa0bff11ff1eef1e2ee548f2653a4da7d2.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(5 - x)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = k(5 - x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(5-x) & =0 \\\\ 5-x & =0 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x = 5 \\, \\rm{m}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $4$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}$ | $k > 0$ | $0$ | $-k <         0$ |\n| $v$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x = 5\\,\\rm{m}.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = k(5 - x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x = \\int k(5 - x) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = k\\left(5x - \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2 \\right) + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = \\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 0 \\, \\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(5x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(5(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = k\\left(5x - \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2 \\right) + 3.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v = 2\\sqrt{6} \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 1 \\, \\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(5x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(5(1)-\\frac{1}{2}{(1)}^{2})+3 \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9k}{2}+3 \\\\ \\frac{9k}{2} & =9 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2(5x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =10x-{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =20x-2{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+20x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\sqrt{-2x^2 + 20x + 6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\nv_{\\max} = v(5) =\\sqrt{-2(5)^2 + 20(5) + 6} = 2\\sqrt{14}\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bbddc22-1d4e-5f0c-8859-c1f9e53a2420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = ± 3m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177148} {:answer/id #uuid "99e3790c-c681-50ef-a84c-a7e7aa54403d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = ± \\frac{5}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177149} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c42b58-3d5e-5946-a04c-58bea74e53a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177150} {:answer/id #uuid "29dd84be-2f6d-5d26-b1a0-4e321b4670a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = ± 1m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177151} {:answer/id #uuid "7cac9eae-aedc-5249-85a2-767758ae250e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177152}] :answer-field/id #uuid "007b3e46-8dad-51c7-8a59-e1310d4ee842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c22ac9-bc43-5e83-8190-be59ba1c762b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364738} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7295fa-6d9e-5d5d-ace2-98292c1e2c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364739} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ec5736-f562-5006-9075-76be4aa2829c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364740} {:answer/id #uuid "613016b7-01e3-59ff-b9fa-3d6967d724ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364741} {:answer/id #uuid "be24e126-1d61-5730-b551-26b869a40e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ec5736-f562-5006-9075-76be4aa2829c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364740} :answer-field/id #uuid "301bd13d-c10c-5781-ac8d-14b89a952bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed248f52-e624-5938-bb17-c2a2073c478d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108003" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $2x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $4\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4b/4b08d915d0adf8bf603070aa96bde1edf0cfb392d9be5f9bd4786897a6c943fa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $2x\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =8+1 \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=9-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832b5d4d-e8f0-59d5-bee8-54cb170284f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178246} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9ee044-4f23-5d1d-937a-299254c22561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178247} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b2b4c7-5396-581c-935e-15122f426131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178248} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3eb59b-f62b-5e45-a8b1-c63b6127a097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178249} {:answer/id #uuid "46fd8948-98a2-5118-b13b-7e3247b7f82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178250}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c6d079-dabb-5dcb-882f-ef5e5c9e1404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10df90f0-7460-5f12-8581-b5f006e2d2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365241} {:answer/id #uuid "f110b675-f1c8-5f63-8220-fc65403fad04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365242} {:answer/id #uuid "2e95e064-0e7e-525f-a9b9-71fc8b392c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365243} {:answer/id #uuid "60f55c07-f52c-5cbf-b071-44b4b630f272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365244} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbc27f4-058f-5b3a-9cd6-5f66a1f934c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10df90f0-7460-5f12-8581-b5f006e2d2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365241} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae01d3c-e097-5248-8e9e-f1b4d1ac9d3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f943434e-0f0c-50a1-bdc1-4d726efdd1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-163492" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/0f/0f8f7773a5e55c81de553eccf684f9a6128dd6e76e06b9040d5953371e72184f.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $1>0$ | $0$ | $-1<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=3\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (3-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =6x-{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(3)=\\sqrt{6(3)-(3)^{2}+4}=\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "804598f5-dd74-5b29-af4e-e2b11d2816d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365248} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d36c18-0514-53d7-b666-88b8e4ccc540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365249} {:answer/id #uuid "161d476d-7776-5af8-b0f7-476e8019ea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365250} {:answer/id #uuid "622773bb-b396-56ef-8151-ec49ee0dacf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365251} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a9689f-0760-5e4d-8295-c6dd2388de80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "622773bb-b396-56ef-8151-ec49ee0dacf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365251} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eec898a-74f2-529d-b4bf-a5628b150053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89d922d3-34d3-536f-bab1-5d011a22c471" :question/math-academy-id "q-163500" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $kx\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=20\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $10\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cd42eb74e5bba02951118c349a1b25f7193c39aa53f6504403918dfa984eb2c2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $kx\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=20\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(20)^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=200-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=10\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =200-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =200-\\frac{k}{2}(10)^{2} \\\\ 50k & =200 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9676d1bd-b5f6-576b-af89-b417c0b3f836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365262} {:answer/id #uuid "acd5375e-9e97-53b6-9073-a053c886f8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365263} {:answer/id #uuid "a553426d-f0bb-5e6d-a4fa-349049cf240a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365264} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa4d97d-b040-5b59-a4e2-7990a8d14150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365265} {:answer/id #uuid "af4992d7-4f53-5694-ab5f-c15156f271e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a553426d-f0bb-5e6d-a4fa-349049cf240a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365264} :answer-field/id #uuid "de8be0bb-c786-552b-8444-d92b5d0e42e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e8a8914-09f4-5fec-89f5-6922293a4aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-163549" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(10-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/33/33b95a6985a53b76eb7f74771825c18f21d1e59b2245325e14690a365a2a8bb0.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(10-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=10-2x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=5\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $4$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=5\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=10-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (10-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=10x-{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =10x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =10(0)-(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =10x-{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =20x-2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+20x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+20x+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(5)=\\sqrt{-2(5)^{2}+20(5)+4}=3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6838b8cf-fec7-51b2-8373-943cee569878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365276} {:answer/id #uuid "8205c3ac-78de-533c-ae96-ff65f5828af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365277} {:answer/id #uuid "c11907f7-3548-559e-a061-4bcaf9c1b34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365278} {:answer/id #uuid "a7869954-b653-5a50-9765-f8085d861bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365279} {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3028a6b-dad4-502a-85ce-856d7efdc2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "346646b9-04ff-58cb-a1cc-6c9b42073112" :question/math-academy-id "q-163551" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m$ and the particle has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\pi \\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b396b80995e34fe348b3d059737b1eec3d4ce5a9d2e9138a8e7d4ed5c0945dc0.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=\\pi \\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ 32 & =8+k \\\\ k & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e23279e-f596-55c4-8ba0-1c3a83d3dcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365311} {:answer/id #uuid "caa8763f-aff6-523f-934f-fa4587c0b1d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365312} {:answer/id #uuid "89e5bb75-58a7-5802-93ed-cbcf8a53e09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365313} {:answer/id #uuid "49d2ffeb-5f5d-5f96-a5a2-96055383edb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365314} {:answer/id #uuid "dc75b7ec-b72d-5ae0-9a65-0e3451d1ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{38}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc75b7ec-b72d-5ae0-9a65-0e3451d1ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{38}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365315} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6cd475a-5481-501e-8c0b-b3e3c26d38cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "017ea912-f935-5953-b22c-217f292a2ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163560" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(8-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/5a/5a1fa0d49dc7a35d176473d3554de6383fa042cc2c5daf7eb5abaad1d5b387cd.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(8-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(8-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(8-2x) & =0 \\\\ 8-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $3$ |  | $5$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(8-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(8x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(8x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(8(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(8x-{x}^{2})+3.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(8x-{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =k(8(1)-{(1)}^{2})+3 \\\\ 10 & =7k+3 \\\\ 7k & =7 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(8x-{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16x-2{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+16x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+16x+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-2(4)^{2}+16(4)+6}=\\sqrt{38}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcef8185-08c1-5b71-bc52-921851bf0b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365318} {:answer/id #uuid "e5275c33-9f19-588a-a1a5-fc9539abdaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365319} {:answer/id #uuid "63a5775d-573d-54a4-a004-5fba51faf6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{19}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365320} {:answer/id #uuid "d868e69f-b2d3-56b5-96c6-dfe006404797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365321} {:answer/id #uuid "d7368030-8d8f-5aa7-a3e5-8ee7271c70e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a5775d-573d-54a4-a004-5fba51faf6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{19}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365320} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa0d68b-3aee-5717-aebf-0211366a9b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fbdf53c-2cfd-519b-93e8-373440a987e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163562" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(6-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/a8/a8ff5e02237554d0f7107168f287adbe1834c4dd7ad965163531f5a23412a4fc.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(6-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(6-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(6-x) & =0 \\\\ 6-x & =0 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=6\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,6)$ | $6$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $5$ |  | $7$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=6\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(6-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(6-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =k(6(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{11}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{11})^{2} & =k(6(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+2 \\\\ 22 & =10k+2 \\\\ 10k & =20 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2(6x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =12x-{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =24x-2{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}+24x+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(6)=\\sqrt{-2(6)^{2}+24(6)+4}=2\\sqrt{19}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3fd877-b517-54cd-8c80-50dcad23f8c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{5}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365338} {:answer/id #uuid "59e5737c-a359-55e7-b3f6-f82aec427d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt[3]{10}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365339} {:answer/id #uuid "69ae8fce-9a01-5a5e-a168-dc8c2ea3731a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2}{5}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365340} {:answer/id #uuid "f743fd6d-e8ee-5651-ac14-9214e4c430c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt[3]{5}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365341} {:answer/id #uuid "10531992-e5ae-593e-8b77-96156a634b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{10}{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59e5737c-a359-55e7-b3f6-f82aec427d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt[3]{10}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365339} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d7f15de-5d1e-5114-bbe1-ed49b8d04ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8462dfb3-4f09-59cb-a378-08b5bbed4fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163571" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $3{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $2\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52d095f73fdaa91c6a2fe9f2caca74929a22ec09618e85395732ea451bda81c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $3{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-{x}^{3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-{x}^{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =-(2)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2+8 \\\\ C & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=10-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =10-{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =10-{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3} & =10 \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{10}\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5eca-1b90-5fe4-9c4b-927d2a442f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365352} {:answer/id #uuid "58cd2a77-5515-5752-ad84-4537d236a6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365353} {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} {:answer/id #uuid "714cdf7d-69ae-5343-9e54-3467a962889f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365355} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8579b3-2d8a-5067-90b8-819ebd380eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a20e2d-0bf7-5a2c-9004-99c10ddffbbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7407b0b-36b6-5f0d-b3ce-2c90d7096c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-163573" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $1\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cdb88c0c679ca9325df42c5c8c4feaea6ce6c0cf02655410158860d7b03d54dc.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{2})+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ 2x(1-x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution of this equation for $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$ is $x=1\\,m.$"}]}
